      housing: "Air conditioning is not a luxury for this breed, it is life support. A Bulldog cannot cool itself efficiently, because a dog sheds heat by moving air across the wet surfaces of the airway and a shortened, obstructed airway does that badly. Keep the dog indoors and climate-controlled through hot months, exercise at dawn and dusk only, and never leave one in a car or a sunroom for any length of time. They are poor swimmers and heavy at the front, so pools need a fence or a cover. Walk on a harness, since pressure on the throat from a collar worsens an airway that is already narrowed.",
      diet: "Measure every meal and keep the dog genuinely lean, because weight is the single biggest modifiable multiplier on both airway obstruction and joint load. Fat deposits in the neck and chest narrow an airway that has no margin to spare, so an overweight Bulldog is a more breathless Bulldog. An elevated slow-feed bowl helps: brachycephalic dogs swallow a lot of air while eating, which contributes to gas and to regurgitation. Food allergies and food-responsive skin disease are common, so a slow, methodical diet trial with your vet is often more useful than switching brands on a hunch.",
      enrichment: "Short, cool, low-intensity sessions. A Bulldog will happily follow you into an activity it physically cannot sustain, so the owner has to be the one who stops it. Watch for noisy breathing that gets louder, a widened stance, blue or brick-red gums, or a dog that lies down and will not get up: those are respiratory distress, not tiredness. Their enrichment can be almost entirely low-impact, with puzzle feeders, short training sessions, and chew work carrying most of the load. They are genuinely fond of children and tolerant of household chaos, which is a real part of why people keep choosing them.",
      health: "This is a breed that requires active medical management rather than routine care. A study of 2,662 English Bulldogs found 2.04 times the odds of being diagnosed with at least one disorder compared with other dogs, with raised odds in 24 of 43 specific disorder categories, and its authors called for the breed standard to be redefined toward a moderate conformation. UK life-table data gives a life expectancy of 7.39 years at birth against 11.23 years for dogs overall. The main issues are brachycephalic obstructive airway syndrome (BOAS), which is the collection of anatomical obstructions caused by a shortened skull; skin fold dermatitis in the facial, tail, and vulvar folds; dental crowding in a jaw too short for a full set of teeth; hip and elbow dysplasia; cherry eye and dry eye; and a birth canal narrow enough that cesarean delivery is the norm rather than the exception. Buy from a breeder who screens, who breathes their dogs on a graded exercise test, and who will talk frankly about all of this.",
